WASHINGTON, Aug. 11 – Sen. Bernie Sanders (I-Vt.), Ranking Member of the Senate Health, Education, Labor, and Pensions (HELP) Committee, today issued the following statement after Yale University researchers released a new study finding that Sanders’ Medicare for All legislation would save 114,000 lives and reduce overall healthcare spending in America by more than $1 trillion a year.

“This study confirms what we have known for years: Medicare for All saves lives and saves money. In fact, guaranteeing healthcare as a human right through a Medicare for All, single-payer system would cost $1 trillion less than our current dysfunctional system. It would save working families thousands of dollars a year. And it would prevent over 100,000 Americans from dying unnecessarily each and every year because they cannot make it to a doctor in time,” Sanders said. “At a time when 15 million Americans are being thrown off the healthcare they have and 20 million Americans have already seen their premiums double, on average, as a result of Trump’s so-called ‘Big Beautiful Bill,’ we need Medicare for All now more than ever. The time is now to end the greed of the big insurance and drug companies and pass Medicare for All.”

Yale University researchers found that Medicare for All would reduce total annual healthcare spending by nearly 20%, or more than $1 trillion while eliminating premiums, deductibles and copayments, provider networks, and prior authorization. As a result, Americans would save:

$377.5 billion by paying no more than other major countries for prescription drugs;

$286.3 billion by eliminating bureaucracy and waste;

$285.7 billion by curbing fraudulent billing;

$100 billion by preventing costly emergency room and hospital visits; and

$295.6 billion by reimbursing providers fairly for the care they provide.

Researchers also estimate that Medicare for All would save 114,000 lives every year, including:

33,232 Americans who are currently uninsured and can’t access lifesaving care;

29,631 Americans who are currently underinsured and cannot afford care;

20,111 Americans who would no longer lose their health insurance as a result of Trump’s “Big Beautiful Bill”;

18,200 seniors who are currently unable to afford their prescription drugs; and

13,000 seniors who would receive quality care in nursing homes.

WASHINGTON—Today, Representative James P. McGovern (D-MA), Ranking Member of the House Rules Committee and Co-Chair of the Tom Lantos Human Rights Commission, wrote to Secretary of State Marco Rubio urging him to center human rights and civilian protection in all United States engagement in the Central Sahel, a region in West Africa that encompasses Mali, Burkina Faso, and Niger.

Citing expert testimony delivered at a May 14 hearing of the Tom Lantos Human Rights Commission, the letter raises alarms about the “precarity and violence of life in the region, which boasts the world’s fastest growing theaters for armed Islamists allied to Al Qaeda and the Islamic State.” It notes that “security forces in the Central Sahel have adopted an aggressive counterterrorism approach and themselves been implicated in widespread violations of international humanitarian law.”

“Human rights violations and war crimes by government forces and their allies are significant drivers of recruitment into armed groups,” McGovern wrote. “For this reason, meaningful improvement in the regional security situation cannot be achieved unless human rights and civilian protection are treated as the non-negotiable foundation of engagement with Mali, Burkina Faso, and Niger.”

The letter calls on Secretary Rubio to:

Ensure that any provision of foreign assistance occurs in strict compliance with human rights conditions in U.S. law;

Designate human rights violators for targeted sanctions under the Global Magnitsky Act;

Provide public explanation of the lifting of sanctions on individuals who could be implicated in serious human rights abuses, such as the February 2026 delisting of three Malian officials linked to Russia's abusive Wagner Group;

Consistent with the Elie Wiesel Act, assess the risk of future atrocities in the Central Sahel and report to Congress on regional atrocity prevention;

Demand the immediate release of arbitrarily detained political figures, journalists, and civil society;

Support inclusive, local peace processes; and

Increase humanitarian and development aid for essential local services to minimize the grievances that extremist groups exploit.

